s of sleep per night. She denies any other review of systems.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological exam is normal. Musculoskeletal exam is normal. Cardiovascular exam shows a grade 2 systolic ejection fraction back ejection murmur. Pulmonary exam is normal. Neck exam is normal.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 24-year-old female with a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension. She presents for her annual exam.\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s hemoglobin A1c is elevated. Her blood sugars are out of control.\n• Additional Testing: I would like her to check her blood sugars in the morning and call them in to me in 2 weeks.\n• Medical Treatment: We will continue her metformin 1000 mg twice a day. She should continue to monitor her blood sugars. I would like her to cut down on snacking and sweets. We will follow up in 2 months.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressures are within normal limits.\n• Medical Treatment: She should continue to take lisinopril 10 mg once a day.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nDiabetes.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Beverly is a 24-year-old female who presents for her yearly exam. The patient reports that she has been doing well. She started working in a bakery this summer which has been super fun. She has really been enjoying working there, but she finds herself, since she is so close to so many delicious treats, snacking a lot during shifts and eating a lot more desserts than she normally would. She has been taking her diabetes medication as prescribed. She has not missed any doses. She states that her diet has been pretty bad lately. She denies any weight loss or gain. She reports that her blood pressures have been good. She has been sleeping well, getting a full eight hours. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideation.