ho presents for a high blood pressure check. She has a history of hypertension and kidney transplant. She has been on Norvasc 10 mg daily. She has had some headaches. Her blood pressures have been elevated. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\nSupple.\n\nRespiratory\nLungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\nIrregular rate and rhythm. S1, S2. Slight 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nGastrointestinal\nLeft flank scar for kidney transplant, healed. No lower extremity edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCreatinine is within normal limits.\n\nEKG is within normal limits.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 26-year-old female with a history of hypertension and kidney transplant who presents for high blood pressure control.\n\nHypertension\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressure is high and the Norvasc 10 mg daily is not controlling it. She reports headaches.\n• Additional Testing: Lipid panel, complete metabolic panel.\n• Medical Treatment: Prescribed carvedilol 25 mg twice a day.\n\nKidney transplant\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well with stable kidney function.\n• Additional Testing: Lipid panel, complete metabolic panel.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current immunosuppression.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSusan Rodriguez is a 26-year-old female who presents for a high blood pressure check. The patient reports that her blood pressure has always been high, but it is now becoming uncontrolled. She was previously on Norvasc 5 mg daily and was increased to 10 mg daily. The patient reports that her blood pressures have been elevated at home and notes that she has had headaches, which she attributes to stress at work or her high blood pressure. The patient d