er to avoid any activities that put extra pressure on her knee for a little while. I advised her to avoid breaststroke and to take a break from swimming if needed. We will see her again in a couple of weeks if her pain does not improve.\n\nFor her chronic back pain, I recommended that she continue to swim. This has been well-tolerated and I do not want to make any changes at this time.\n\nFor her depression, I recommended that we continue her current medication regimen. She has been doing well with her therapy and I do not want to make any changes at this time.\n\nFor her history of carpal tunnel release, I recommended that we continue to monitor her. She reports that she have been doing well and I do not want to make any changes at this time.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks if her pain does not improve.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nRebecca Johnson is a 27-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The patient was last seen in clinic 6 months ago for carpal tunnel syndrome. The patient reports that she has been swimming more regularly over the course of the month and has been trying to get active. She notes that she is able to swim 10 laps initially, but after a couple of laps she starts to feel pain in her right knee. The patient denies any specific injury to the knee. The patient reports that her back pain has been doing well.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\nPsychiatric: Endorses depression.\nNeurological: Denies pain or numbness in legs.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or palpitations.\nRespiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath, or chest pain.\nSkin: Denies rashes, joint pain, or swelling.\nNeurological: Denies headaches, seizures, or weakness.\nNutrition: Denies weight loss or gain or changes in appetite.\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that she has been swimming more frequently.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that she is taking medication for her depression.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies changes in appetite or weight loss.\nEyes: Denies changes in vision.\nHENT: Denies sore throat, sinusitis, or hearing loss.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or palpitations.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\nGenitourinary: Denies urinary incontinence.\nNeurological: Denies headaches, weakness, or numbness.\nPsychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nVITALS\n\nTemperature: 98.1 degrees Fahrenheit.\nBlood pressure: 114/70 mm Hg.\nRespiratory rate: 20 breaths per minute.\nHeart rate: 60 beats per minute.\nOxygen saturation: 98% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No carotid bruits, rubs, or gallops. Normal heart sounds. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\n\nNEURO\n- Orientation: Normal\n- Cranial nerves: 2/2\n- Reflexes: Normal\n- Sensation: Normal\n- Coordination: Normal\n\nMSK\n- Inspection: No swelling or erythema of the right knee\n- Palpation: No tenderness over the right knee\n- Range of motion: Painful\n- Special tests: Positive patellar inhibition\n- Strength: 5/5 in the right lower extremity\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee is unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right knee pain, most likely secondary to chondromalacia of the patella.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is a swimmer and has been increasing her swimming activities. She reports pain in her right knee after swimming. She has a history of carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nI advised the patient to take ibuprofen 600 mg four times daily with food. I advised her to avoid any activity that puts extra pressure on her knee. I advised her to do straight leg raising exercises. I advised her to avoid lunges and breaststroke until her pain improves. She can try other swimming strokes where her knees are straight. I advised her to follow up with me if her pain does not improve in a couple of weeks.\n\n2. Chronic back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her back pain.\n\n3. Depression.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her depression.\n\n4. Carpal tunnel syndrome.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Thompson is a 27 y.o. female with a history of carpal tunnel release done a couple years ago, chronic back pain, and depression. She presents today for joint pain.\n\nMs. Thompson reports that over the course of the month she has begun swimming more regularly and trying to be more active. She has experienced bad right knee pain. The patient reports that her knee starts hurting after a couple of laps. She notes previously she could swim 10 laps. She denies injury or trauma to the knee.\n\nThe patient has a history of chronic back pain that has been intermittent, but right now it has been doing well. She denies pain radiating down her legs.\n\nThe patient has a history of depression, which has been going really well with medication and therapy. She is trying to get outside more and it has been helping her with her depression. She notes she is sleeping and eating well. She denies concerns about hurting herself or others.\n\nThe patient\'s carpal tunnel is doing really well. She has been getting full range of motion and it has felt a lot better.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses right knee joint pain. Endorses chronic back pain.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Neurological/Psychological: Appropriate mood and affect. Grips are equal bilaterally.\n• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n• Musculoskeletal: No lower extremity edema. Pain with patellar inhibition. Pain with range of motion of the right knee.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee is unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMs. Thompson is a 27 y.o. female who presents today for evaluation of right knee pain.\n\nChondromalacia of right patella.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been more active lately but is unaware of any injury to the knee. Her recent x-rays were normal but she did have some discomfort on exam.\n• Medical Treatment: Prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food. I do want her to do some straight leg raising exercises that will strengthen her quadriceps.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged her to participate in physical activity that is less straining on her joints , such as swimming freestyle laps, or to stop activity all together. If her pain does not improve in a couple of weeks, she will follow up.\n\nChronic back pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is stable and well-controlled at this time.\n• Medical Treatment: She should continue swimming regularly once her knee is feeling better.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with her current regimen.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current medications.\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome.\n• Medical Reasoning: Relatively stable status post carpal tunnel release.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current regimen with no changes at this time.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
