not sure if it\'s type i or type ii . even patients in , you know , in their mid-twenties can develop type i diabetes , so we\'re gon na go off and , we\'re gon na go ahead and , and send some labs to work that up . hey , dragon ? order a complete metabolic panel . and i\'d like to start you on metformin , 500 milligrams , twice a day . that will help keep your blood sugars down .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] hey , dragon ? order metformin , 500 milligrams , twice a day . and then if some of the autoimmune labs that come back show that you have an autoimmune disorder , meaning that you would have type i diabetes , then we would have to go ahead and put you on insulin . but i\'m gon na hold off until we have those results , okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] but in the meantime , i really want you to avoid , uh , foods high in sugar . um , and the , the nurse will come in and she\'ll give you a glucometer and teach you how to do blood sugar testing . okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] and then , from an atrial fibrillation standpoint , your heart , even though you\'re in atrial fibrillation right now , is in a good rate . and we\'ll just continue you on your metoprolol , 25 milligrams , twice a day . do you need a refill on that ?\n[patient] yes , i do .\n[doctor] hey , dragon ? order a refill on metoprolol , 25 milligrams , twice a day . and for your reflux , we\'ll just continue you on the protonix , okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] do you have any questions , hannah ?\n[patient] no , not at this time .\n[doctor] okay , so the nurse will come in and she\'ll go over all that stuff with you .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] take care .\n[patient] thank you .\n[doctor] hey , dragon ? finalize the note .\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood sugar.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nHannah Smith is a 52-year-old female who presents for evaluation of high blood sugar. The patient states she has been eating a lot of sweets recently with the holidays. She reports that she has been eating a lot of sweets at work and with family. She states she felt fine initially, but after a couple of weeks she started to feel lightheaded and was seeing spots. She states she went into the emergency room and they said she had a high blood sugar. She reports that she was given fluids in the emergency room. She states she was told to cut out sweets and get back into her normal routine and diet. The patient states she is still staying with her family, and her mother and grandmother are big bakers, so she thinks it will be hard to get into a routine.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain or burning with urination.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle aches.\n• Neurological: Endorses lightheadedness and seeing spots.\n• Endocrine: Endorses high blood sugar.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Constitutional: Appears in no distress.\n• Neck: Thyroid is slightly enlarged and non-tender. No carotid bruits.\n• Cardiovascular: Irregular rate and rhythm.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear.\n• Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft.\n• Musculoskeletal: No edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c is elevated.\nBlood glucose is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Diagnosis of diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood sugar and hemoglobin A1c levels are elevated. She has a family history of diabetes.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient on the importance of avoiding foods high in sugar.\n• Medical Treatment: I am going to start the patient on metformin 500 mg twice a day. If some of the autoimmune labs show that she has an autoimmune disorder, meaning she would have type 1 diabetes, then we will have to start her on insulin.\n• Additional Testing: We are going to order a complete metabolic panel.\n\n2. Atrial 